Subject: [PATCH] decodeString: decode ISO-8859-1, not UTF-8

The CCU's interface processes speak ISO-8859-1. `decodeString()` ends in
`strContent.toString()`, which is UTF-8, so every byte above 0x7F comes back as
U+FFFD and the character is unrecoverable: a `°C` unit from rfd or a `Tür` in a
device name arrives as replacement characters.

The encoder is already right. `encodeString()` uses `Buffer.from(str, 'ascii')`,
which in node keeps the low byte of each code unit - the same bytes `latin1`
would write - so `0xFC` goes out as `0xFC` and only comes back wrong. Measured
on a CCU3 (firmware 3.89.8) by writing `Tür Küche äöüß °C` into device metadata
and reading the raw bytes back over both transports.

`latin1` is what the protocol is: binrpc is a HomeMatic-specific library and
every peer that speaks it is an eQ-3 interface process. If a per-instance option
is preferred to a fixed encoding, the equivalent change is to thread an
`encoding` option from `Client`/`Server` down to `decodeString` and default it
to `'latin1'`; the one-line form below is what this application needs and what
it patches locally today.

Verified against binrpc 4.2.0.

---
 lib/protocol.js | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/lib/protocol.js b/lib/protocol.js
index 0000000..1111111 100644
--- a/lib/protocol.js
+++ b/lib/protocol.js
@@ -233,7 +233,9 @@
             strContent: strContent,
             rest: elem.subarray(4 + strContent.length),
         };
-        str.content = str.strContent.toString();
+        // The CCU's interface processes speak ISO-8859-1 and send no encoding
+        // declaration; `toString()` would decode UTF-8 and lose every byte > 0x7F.
+        str.content = str.strContent.toString('latin1');
         return str;
     },
     /**
